          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"verona-colei-che-non-si-deve-amare-739102cf42485872\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"“Amo te” is principally a deliberate avowal of Loretta’s love, strongly supporting T. In immediate coordination with “voglio esser tua” and her proposed shared life, it also helps enact an offer of herself and an undertaking toward a lovers’ relationship, supporting P. Her explicit insistence that she is reasoning calmly and knows what she is saying argues against construing the words as an expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is whether the substantial undertaking force belongs to “Amo te” itself or chiefly to the coordinated continuation “voglio esser tua”; the unified declaration gives P significant but lower support than T.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Loretta directly tells Arrigo “Amo te” (“I love you”) during an extended attempt to persuade him to accept a sexual and romantic relationship.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Loretta addresses the man identified as her brother and consciously rejects his moral effort to resist their mutual attraction. The avowal is part of a sustained, persuasive declaration of autonomy: she claims responsibility for seeking him, accepting remorse, becoming his, and living beside him. The taboo, erotic pressure, and persuasive purpose complicate the scene but do not displace the utterance’s avowing and undertaking forces. Her claim to calm deliberation especially disfavors E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Guarda: ragiono freddamente, so quel che dico. Amo te” (“Look: I am reasoning calmly; I know what I am saying. I love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit love avowal strongly supports T, while the asserted calm deliberation weighs against reflex-like E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Amo te, voglio esser tua; solo questo mi piace.”\",\"supports\":\"The coordination of loving him with wanting to be his gives the declaration substantial relational-undertaking force and supports P alongside T.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Loretta continues: “Voglio carezzarti, farmi carezzare, viverti vicino, essere innamorata di te, gelosa di te...”\",\"supports\":\"Her concrete projection of intimacy and life near him shows that the avowal participates in a proposal to enter and sustain a lovers’ relationship, rather than merely reporting an isolated feeling.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“E son io che ti cerco, non tu... io lo voglio portare tutto su me stessa” and later “E son io che voglio, non tu; io sola.”\",\"supports\":\"Her repeated assertion of agency and willingness to bear the remorse reinforces the deliberate, undertaking-oriented interpretation.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Arrigo says “Perchè sei mia sorella,” while Loretta argues that she does not experience him as her brother.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the incest taboo and persuasive conflict as crucial context without creating a core force outside T/P/E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence metadata identifies an affirmative present-tense main-or-embedded form and reviews it as a valid explicit I-love-you occurrence.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates treating “Amo te” as the target explicit love declaration rather than an incidental lexical use.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the deliberate emotional avowal and its role in proposing a committed lovers’ relationship. Intensity, taboo, sexuality, and persuasion are adequately handled as context; there is no residual core-force pressure requiring O, and no positive evidence for E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
